<blockquote>
Recruiting the Best: Recruiting for a Gameless Guild<br />
Written by <a href="mailto:mayhem@conclaveofguilds.com">Mayhem</a><br />
</br>
This being the first feature article in our Recruiting the Best series, we
need a topic which is appropriate.  You may have noticed the "theme"
recurrent throughout these initial articles.  Well, rest assured, it's been
intentional and will be continued within this piece.  After all, what is
more appropriate to launching this new resource than talking to our readers
about what to do before they ever step foot into the Norrath of the future?<br />
<br />
We should start of with a quick bit about the task of recruitment and it's
role in guilds.  The way recruitment is viewed and handled within guilds
varies.  In part this is due to the character of the guild and the
personality of the members, but also has to do with the structuring of the
guild charter.  In some guilds this is very loose: "Prove 'x' and you're
in."  In this case, 'x' may be things like your level, your attitude, your
skill, your class, or even just who your friends are.  In other guilds there
are long (sometimes convoluted) processes to being recruited.  As well, who
handles the recruitment of new members is highly variable.  Some Guild
Leaders reserve this duty for themselves (particularly in small guilds),
while others let almost anyone add members (as almost every member is made
an officer shortly after entry), and still others provide specific
"recruitment officers" with the discretion to guild applicants.  The bottom
line is to find something that works for your guild, and to be sure that
whatever that something is, it's standardized to some extent.<br />
<br />
For the purposes of this article, we'll assume that there two people focused
on recruiting new members before the release of the game are the Guild
Leader, as well as one "recruiting officer" who was chosen by the Guild
Leader to fulfill this role.  This may or may not be the method chosen by
new guilds forming, but it gives us a point of reference.  Adapt what 
follows in this article as you see fit, bearing in mind the way your specific
guild is structured.<br />
<br />
In the beginning there is one.  That one is the Guild Leader, and from there
the guild starts to grow.  The initial recruiting process is handled in
various manners, depending on the contacts the Guild Leader has, or makes.
Typical locations for recruiting people are forums, web sites,
friends/family, and other online games.  For the initial stages, the Guild
Leader needs to ensure that each person brought into the budding
organization will be a good fit for the guild as a whole.  In some cases,
this means not recruiting your brother who just turned 14, likes to use
timing cheats in Counterstrike, and is generally a rude little bugger . . .
even if he is family.  Then again, if the rest of the guild fits that
profile, go ahead!  (It's an example, I'm not trying to alienate you
teenagers, I was one once too, even if it was before the prevalence of 
internet gaming.)<br />
<br />
Once the initial folks are starting to come together, the Guild Leader needs
to find out what roles they would like to assist in.  As noted in this
month's Leading the Masses column, avoid making people an "officer-for-life"
just based upon being an early adopter.  Provisional officer status is
certainly in line for those who put a good deal of effort into a young
guild, and can give you a core of officers to begin with until a bit after
release.  (This topic will be covered more in-depth in the next Leading the
Masses column, as we look at how to write a good charter.)  One important
duty to be allocated at this time is one of recruitment.  The individual
tasked with the oversight of this process should be a provisional officer
(for reasons which become more apparent later in this article) and needs to
be prepared to conduct screening of those interested in the guild.<br />
<br />
A screening and recruitment process should be formed, perhaps by the members
as a whole, or perhaps with just a smaller selection such as the Guild
Leader and recruitment officer.  This should be comprehensive, and including
both the pre-release induction process as well as the projected (always
allow for the fact that new ideas may seem better once you're actually
playing) in-game process.  Different things which can be used as a part of
the screening process are discussed more below, but it's vital that there be
some kind of structure as it will alleviate "accidental lapses of judgement"
which can allow people who are not suited to your guild to join up.  In
addition, setting a standard allows you to eliminate (or at least minimize)
any feelings of inequity which might result from immediately accepting some
people, while grilling others before offering up a seemingly reluctant
probationary period.  (It certainly is possible to alienate people in this
manner, which is why in a new guild, everyone should start at the same
place.)<br />
<br />
We'll focus first on the pre-release screening process as it is most
relevant to the subject of this article.  Remember that much of this process
will be governed by the recruitment officer.  When reading the ideas below,
bear in mind that each guild is individual, and what is a good criteria for
one guild may not be for the next.  In no particular order, here are some
ideas to consider when setting up your guild's process:<br />
<ul>
<li>A questionnaire to be filled out by each potential new member, including:<br />
a) A series of questions relating to the person's (projected) play schedule.
Players in the UK will not usually have the same play schedule as those on
the west coast of the US as they are offset by a full (8 hour)shift.<br />
b) Questions relating to what "types of play" a potential member prefers.
Powergamers don't always get along with roleplayers after all. Even barring
personality conflicts, simply the disparity in level between a large group
of casual gamers and a couple of powergamers will almost ensure that there
is a rift in the future.  (Though a mix of hardcore and casual can work,
assuming the balance is good enough, so don't limit yourself to recruiting
only those with a particular style.)<br />
c) A line of questioning designed to determine the prior experience of the
player.  A group of Everquest veterans may not be interested in taking on
someone whose prior game experience consists of nothing but real time
strategy gaming.
</li>
<li>
A gaming session (typically online) with the potential recruit(s) and
some of the current (and preferably trustworthy) guild members.  This could
be anything from creating a new "test group" of low level Everquest
characters to playing a rousing night of Quake ]|[.  The goal is to see what
these folks are like to play with, since being in a guild is best when the
members generally have a good time in each other's company.
</li>
<li>
An email, chat, or messenger "interview" between the recruitment officer,
Guild Leader and potential recruit.  This allows for a bit more free-form
discussion than the questionnaire, and could be used as a "second step" or
to gather more information regarding answers given previously.
</li>
<li>
A "trial period" during which the provisional member is allowed access to
private guild forums, chat meetings, etc.  This is in fact a good plan for
all members recruited prior to release.  It can take a bit of time to
discern things about someone when you're not actively playing with them.
Placing them in a probationary state for a pre-defined period can give you
more of an opportunity to observe them long-term.
</li>
<li>
Particularly focused on roleplaying guilds (but also useful just in
proving dedication in other types of guilds) is the idea of requiring a
short story about the game world or characters in it.  A background story
for the type of character the player plans on taking on the role of.
Perhaps a tale of a past adventure or humorous anecdote.  This is a
requirement designed to test the creativity of an individual.  (Also bear in
mind that a roleplaying chat is a useful screening tool!)
</li>
<li>
Lastly, there may simply be an agreement to recruit everyone from within
a subset of another game.  For example, if you are starting an Everquest 2
guild, and you're doing so with 10 or 20 other people from your current
Everquest guild, your recruitment strategy may be simply to get people from
that pool, and expand only after release.  (This is actually the way many
guilds in Everquest 2 will start, and not all of them will carry their names
over.)
</li>
</ul>
Before moving onwards, a quick list of ideas (to be used in addition to, or
instead of, the above) for screening and recruiting after release follows:<br />
<br />
<ul>
<li>
A minimum level requirement.<br>
It's not uncommon for guilds (even shortly after a server comes online) to
set a requirement that mimics the median level of their member body.  In
terms of powergamer guilds, this can start at 10 and work it's way up to
dizzying heights very quickly.  In Everquest 2 with the higher level
requirement, do not be surprised to see guilds with minimum requirements
of 80 or 90 within the first few months.  For more casual guilds it will
probably be common to set a requirement of somewhere between 5 and 30,
just so that people applying have proven they have some modest level of
knowledge.
</li>
<li>
A grouping requirement.<br />
Exceedingly common, this simply means that the potential member must seek
out current guild members to form a group with. Some guilds require a
certain period of time, while others have it vary depending on the
individuals involved (both in the guild and new blood) and the feedback
received from the experience.  Regardless of how this requirement is
defined, most guilds (should at least) consider the feedback from these
sessions when making a decision about an applicant.
</li>
</i>
A personal quest to be completed before or after being guilded.<br />
In some cases this is a matter of the "final step" before a guild accepts
you. Other guilds view it as one of the first things a new member should do
after joining the guild.  This method is rarely used by powergaming guilds,
and is not overly common even amongst roleplaying guilds.  Primarily those
guilds who utilize this form of requirement view it as further proof of
dedication, as well as offering an end result that suits the new member or
guild admirably.
</li>
<li>
Required donations or dues.<br />
Some guilds expect something to be added to the guild treasury or stockpiles
on a one-time or intermittent basis.  Again, an uncommon practice in Everquest,
this may become more prevalent if Everquest 2 features rent to be paid on that
prime guild real-estate.  Guilds that require this typically have either a set
sum or pro-rata (by level) scheme of dues.  It may also include some form of
equipment donation upon acceptance to the guild.
</li>
<li>
A probationary period.<br />
This is designed so that should there be any issue with a new member,
there's no long process attached to resolving it.  It helps guilds which
find themselves perhaps vulnerable (particularly if they have a relatively
light screening process) to recruiting "bad apples" by giving them a very
simple out.  The guild leadership merely decides that a member is not
appropriate for the guild and they remove them.  Using a probationary period
for this ensures that long-term guild members do not feel the fear of being
arbitrarily deguilded, as a different (more representative) process affects
them.
</li>
</ul>
Having defined the process, the recruitment officer is responsible for
carrying out finding potential new guild members.  A good deal of time will
be spent scouring locations in an attempt to get a strong initial following.
The reason this may be important is due to the fact that a guild which can
start out with 30-40 people ready to go from the time a server comes online
will enjoy some significant benefits.  In brief, you will not only have
potential group mates readily at hand, others will be drawn to a
well-represented guild.  It is a sign of success to have a strong following
immediately after release.  Guilds of this nature (assuming the members were
recruited in the right manner) typically outlast those which are formed
after a server is brought online.<br />
<br />
Just to rehash, some of the places which may hold potential recruits
include:
<ul>
<li>
Various <a href="http://forums.conclaveofguilds.com/phpBB2/index.php">forums</a>, in particular those dedicated to Everquest 2.
</li>
<li>
Friends and family members who are interested in joining.
</li>
<li>
Individuals you met while playing other online games, including the
original Everquest.
</li>
<li>
Websites which are dedicated to gaming, MMORPGs, or the Everquest series.
(Sometimes these have resources outside of forums, thus I've mentioned them
separately.)
</li>
<li>
Genre face-to-face meetings, from SCA events to Trek conventions and
everything in between.  (With hundreds of thousands of players,
Everquest-interested folk will pop up anywhere and everywhere.)
</li>
</ul>
There is one more (very major) source of new members which should be
examined.  There have been a number of very successful guilds formed solely
from people who have played in game Beta programs together.  There is a
distinct advantage lent to any guild which has a presence inside the Beta
program.  Whoever has been accepted may act in the capacity of liason for
the guild.  A well-prepared guild has a good deal to offer non-guilded Beta
players, while those same Beta players may have valuable skills which come
from playing the game prior to it's release.  Relationships forged during
participation in the Beta program are a valuable resource which should be
taken advantage of whenever possible.<br />
<br />
Then, finally, once all has been done, the day will come when the game
actually opens, the servers actually come online, and manna falls from the
heavens . . . or something like that.  The recruitment officer as well as
the Guild Leader need to be proactive in making sure everyone has the
information they need to get into the game correctly, the very first time.
This includes (but may not be limited to) knowing where the game will be
available, advertising to the members which server to join, and making sure
that each person is a knowledgable as possible regarding whatever facts are
public knowledge before release.  (Though some individuals may wish to know
little or nothing, it is less likely that those players will have gone about
the process of joining a guild.)<br />
<br />
Between the two of them, getting the guild officially recognized will be a
high priority.  We know nothing about the process of forming a guild at this
point, so it's not possible to do anything more than speculate.  However
they will need to coordinate times for people to be online and in a specific
place (be it a chat lounge or a particular zone) bearing in mind the
realities of play schedules and post-release exploratory frenzy.  Once the
guild has been recognized, it is the responsibility of these two to make
sure each member of the guild is added as quickly as possible so business
may proceed in-game as smoothly (hopefully) as it had prior to release.<br />
<br />
Now we've brought a guild from one person with a vision to a (perhaps)
teeming mass of eager early-adopters.  In the next issue of Recruiting the
Best we plan to look at some screening requirements, and see if there isn't an
idea or two which you'll find useful in setting up your own process to carry
you through to the release of Everquest 2.<br />
<br />
</blockquote>
